1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,067
Let's find out how much one bag of lab materials weighs.

2
00:00:04,067 --> 00:00:06,800
Can we do it without opening the bag?

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,733
The left pan has two bags of the same weight, and three small weights.

4
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:13,567
There are eleven weights on the right.

5
00:00:13,767 --> 00:00:16,433
Each small weight is one unit.

6
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:19,000
Let x be the weight of one bag.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,100
The scales are balanced, so both sides have the same total weight.

8
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:27,633
So we can write that as two x plus three equals eleven.

9
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:30,833
What if I just take the three weights off the left side?

10
00:00:31,033 --> 00:00:32,067
Oh!

11
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:33,567
The right side has dropped.

12
00:00:33,567 --> 00:00:35,433
Now the two sides aren't balanced.

13
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:37,700
Let's put those weights back.

14
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,300
How can we keep both sides balanced?

15
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,967
Take three weights off each side.

16
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:45,700
That's subtracting three from both sides.

17
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:49,100
Two bags on the left, eight weights on the right.

18
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:51,067
So two x equals eight.

19
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:54,333
Two bags weigh eight units altogether.

20
00:00:54,333 --> 00:00:55,967
So how much does one bag weigh?

21
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:59,233
Split each side into two equal groups.

22
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:02,067
Then compare one group from each side.

23
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:04,967
One bag balances four weights.

24
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:06,967
So x equals four.

25
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:11,967
We changed the amounts on both sides, but the two sides stayed equal.

26
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:16,667
We can add or subtract the same number on both sides.

27
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:21,100
We can also multiply or divide both sides by the same non-zero number.

28
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:22,800
That keeps the equation true.

29
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,200
Why does the number have to be non-zero?

30
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,133
Division by zero is undefined.

31
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:34,967
Multiplying both sides by zero leaves us with zero equals zero.

32
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:38,400
That tells us nothing about the original value of x.

33
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:42,267
Now let's try a fraction equation.

34
00:01:42,267 --> 00:01:44,767
The denominators are numbers.

35
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:46,867
Neither of them contains x.

36
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:50,500
The quantity x plus one, divided by three,

37
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,700
plus x divided by two, equals seven.

38
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,767
How can we get rid of the denominators?

39
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,067
The lowest common multiple of three and two is six.

40
00:02:01,067 --> 00:02:03,800
So we multiply both sides by six.

41
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,000
That will clear both denominators.

42
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:12,533
I get two times the quantity x plus one, plus three x, equals seven.

43
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:15,733
Working that out gives x equals one.

44
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:18,133
But did you multiply the right side by six?

45
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:22,200
Let's check, rather than just trusting the working.

46
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,933
Put one back into the original equation.

47
00:02:25,367 --> 00:02:29,067
The left side is two thirds plus one half.

48
00:02:29,067 --> 00:02:30,867
That's seven sixths.

49
00:02:30,867 --> 00:02:33,267
It isn't equal to seven on the right!

50
00:02:33,733 --> 00:02:34,700
Oh!

51
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:37,233
I only multiplied the left side by six.

52
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:38,567
I changed the equation!

53
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:42,933
Let's start again from the original equation.

54
00:02:42,933 --> 00:02:47,600
Multiply every term on the left by six, and the seven on the right too.

55
00:02:48,067 --> 00:02:50,867
Six divided by three is two.

56
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:53,800
Six divided by two is three.

57
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,467
And on the right, six times seven is forty-two.

58
00:02:57,467 --> 00:03:02,467
So two times the quantity x plus one, plus three x, equals forty-two.

59
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:06,267
Multiply every term inside the brackets by two.

60
00:03:06,267 --> 00:03:10,033
That gives two x plus two, plus three x.

61
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:14,733
Collect like terms: five x plus two equals forty-two.

62
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:17,467
Subtract two from both sides.

63
00:03:17,467 --> 00:03:19,233
Five x equals forty.

64
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:22,267
Then divide both sides by five.

65
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:23,967
So x equals eight.

66
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:28,933
We still need to check our answer in the original equation.

67
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:31,767
Checking only the last step isn't enough.

68
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:34,167
Put eight in for x.

69
00:03:34,167 --> 00:03:38,500
On the left, add eight and one, then divide by three.

70
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,100
Then add eight divided by two.

71
00:03:41,533 --> 00:03:44,900
That's three plus four, which equals seven.

72
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:46,300
Both sides are equal.

73
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,733
Our answer checks out!

74
00:03:48,833 --> 00:03:50,233
Your turn.

75
00:03:50,233 --> 00:03:52,867
X minus two equals five.

76
00:03:52,867 --> 00:03:56,400
What should we do to both sides to leave x on its own?

77
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,133
Add two to both sides!

78
00:04:00,133 --> 00:04:01,833
So x equals seven.

79
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:07,233
Check it in the original equation: seven minus two equals five.

80
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:08,900
Both sides are equal.

81
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,933
Our scales use positive weights.

82
00:04:12,933 --> 00:04:16,000
But algebra also works with negatives.

83
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,433
We can still do the same operation on both sides.

84
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:23,300
For example, negative two x equals six.

85
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:26,033
Divide by negative two on both sides.

86
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:28,200
That gives x equals negative three.

87
00:04:28,667 --> 00:04:32,367
Negative two times negative three equals six.

88
00:04:32,367 --> 00:04:33,700
That checks out too.

89
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:36,467
Back to our bags.

90
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:39,000
Each bag weighs four units.

91
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,333
Let's put that value back into the original balance.

92
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:47,167
Two bags plus three weights: two times four plus three equals eleven!

93
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:50,300
Do the same operation on both sides.

94
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:53,333
Multiply or divide by a non-zero number.

95
00:04:53,333 --> 00:04:58,367
For fractions, multiply every term on both sides by the lowest common multiple.

96
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:01,200
Then always check your answer in the original equation.